Q: Trying to understand function fma() and its special features A: <p>First off, a tangible example:</p>
<pre><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;  // for printf
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt; // for rng
#include &lt;math.h&gt;   // for fma

int main(void)
{   int i;

    // seed the rng
    srand(0);

    for (i = 0; i &lt; 10; ++i)
    {   // random doubles in [-500, 500]
        double a = ((double)rand() / RAND_MAX) * 1000.0 - 500.0;
        double b = ((double)rand() / RAND_MAX) * 1000.0 - 500.0;
        double c = ((double)rand() / RAND_MAX) * 1000.0 - 500.0;

        double normal = a * b + c;
        double fused = fma(a, b, c);

        if (normal != fused)
        {   printf(&quot;           a = %.99lg\n&quot;, a);
            printf(&quot;           b = %.99lg\n&quot;, b);
            printf(&quot;           c = %.99lg\n&quot;, c);
            printf(&quot;   a * b + c = %.99lg\n&quot;, normal);
            printf(&quot;fma(a, b, c) = %.99lg\n&quot;, fused);
            printf(&quot;\n&quot;);
        }

    }

    return 0;
}

/*
           a = 298.440033476073267593164928257465362548828125
           b = 411.6473579367842603460303507745265960693359375
           c = -302.44863070661602932887035422027111053466796875
   a * b + c = 122549.60265228440403006970882415771484375
fma(a, b, c) = 122549.602652284389478154480457305908203125

           a = -358.39744464419663927401416003704071044921875
           b = 106.9688762570586959554930217564105987548828125
           c = -483.6994283756704362531309016048908233642578125
   a * b + c = -38821.0713353667888441123068332672119140625
fma(a, b, c) = -38821.07133536678156815469264984130859375

           a = -257.113229370263070450164377689361572265625
           b = -362.76842321398129342924221418797969818115234375
           c = 304.17675422699039700091816484928131103515625
   a * b + c = 93576.737560332039720378816127777099609375
fma(a, b, c) = 93576.73756033202516846358776092529296875

           a = -343.32091074591545520888757891952991485595703125
           b = -99.05560575381650778581388294696807861328125
           c = -370.20955321854427211292204447090625762939453125
   a * b + c = 33637.6512286700890399515628814697265625
fma(a, b, c) = 33637.6512286700817639939486980438232421875
*/
</code></pre>
<p>From my testing, <code>fma(a, b, c)</code> gives different results from <code>a * b + c</code> roughly 25% of the time for float (fmaf), double (fma), and long double (fmal). It is also roughly 32% faster (though this depends on hardware).</p>
<p>Why? <strong>Because<code>fma(a, b, c)</code> is one floating point operation, whereas <code>a * b + c</code> is two.</strong></p>
<p>Every floating point operation always introduces some rounding, because it cannot store the result fully accurately. Even Python will tell you that <code>0.1 + 0.2 = 0.30000000000000004</code>. This is due to the nature of floating point representation, not a fault of the programming language. When we perform <code>a * b + c</code>, we first compute <code>a * b</code> , store the result (first rounding), then we compute <code>result + c</code> and store the final result (second rounding). fma is a special <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floating-point_unit" rel="nofollow noreferrer">FPU</a> instruction that can perform the whole expression as a single floating point operation. So when we perform <code>fma(a, b, c)</code>, It only stores the result once, so it rounds off only once. (<s>The difference is at most 1 ulp because they differ by one rounding step.</s> The difference can be significant in edge cases.)</p>
<p>fma gives better precision for things like polynomials <code>fma(a, x², fma(b, x, c))</code> or for dot products <code>fma(a1, b1, fma(a2, b2, fma(...)))</code>, for example. It is also used for its faster speed in GPUs or in DSP applications a lot.</p>
<p>Although it <em>is</em> better.. I wouldn't recommend using fma in general code because it makes it harder to read. Besides, with the proper settings, your compiler will check for such things, and replace a * b + c with fma for you anyway.</p>

Q: How to make a generic specifiable associated type in Swift? A: <p>Your exact formulation isn't allowed in Swift because the <em>full</em> type of an <code>associatedtype</code> must be known at adoption time (and <code>Collection&lt;X&gt;</code> for an unspecified <code>X</code> isn't valid). If you don't care what <code>X</code> actually is at any point, then @JoakimDanielson's answer is the simplest and easiest - but if you do need to constrain <code>X</code> in some way, then you can incorporate it into the protocol by making <code>SomeProtocol</code> itself generic on <code>X</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>protocol SomeProtocol&lt;X&gt; { // X is the primary associatedtype
    associatedtype X
    associatedtype T: Collection where T.Element == X
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then, to adopt:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>struct SomeStruct&lt;X&gt;: SomeProtocol {
    typealias T = Array&lt;X&gt;
}

struct OtherStruct&lt;T: SomeProtocol&lt;String&gt;&gt; {
    let value: T.T
}
</code></pre>
<p>In use:</p>
<pre class="lang-swift prettyprint-override"><code>let s: OtherStruct&lt;SomeStruct&lt;String&gt;&gt; = .init(value: [&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;])
print(s) // =&gt; OtherStruct&lt;SomeStruct&lt;String&gt;&gt;(value: [&quot;a&quot;, &quot;b&quot;, &quot;c&quot;])
</code></pre>
